    "body": "This is tricky. Doing what you suggest:\r\n\r\n1. Would require moving a few steps around, but that's easy. (This is because _responseCOOP_ is currently computed **after** _responsePolicyContainer_ is created)\r\n2. Doesn't seem to have equivalent behavior to what the current spec does, or what this PR initially attempts to preserve in the current spec. For example, the current spec unconditionally uses _responseCOOP_ regardless of which policy container is selected by \"determine the navigation params policy container\". But if we limit the usage of _responseCOOP_ to the usage of _responsePolicyContainer_, then whenever \"determining the navigation params policy container\" chooses a policy container **other than** _responsePolicyContainer_, we'd be deviating from the current spec. Maybe the current spec is bad though!\r\n\r\nThe cases where \"determining a navigation params policy container\" would _not_ choose _responsePolicyContainer_ is:\r\n - ✅ When _historyPolicyContainer_ is non-null: this is only when we're traversing to a local-scheme session history entry, I believe. In that case, the current spec *always* uses a default-initialized COOP which is probably wrong. Probably we should be honoring/preserving the COOP in the session history entry (via the history policy container after this PR I guess) and using that one.\r\n - ❌ about:srcdoc case; this is handled separately by the srcdoc navigation flow so we don't have to think about it inside #create-navigation-params-by-fetching\r\n - ✅ _Navigation_ (not traversal) to local-scheme documents. In that case, like the above, the current spec *always* uses a default-initialized COOP which is probably wrong. It feels like we should be inheriting the one from the initiator like the rest of the policy container.\r\n\r\nI'll continue this discussion on the main comment thread.",
